# Explicit king-and-pawn solver: rules and file formats

Two solvers must implement this spec: the C solver `kings/kpsolve.c` and an independent Python
reference solver `kings/ref/`. They are compared position by position, so every rule below is
binding. Neither may read the other's code.

## Board

- W files (2 to 8) by H ranks (4 to 8). Files are named a, b, c, ... and ranks 1, 2, ..., H.
- **flat:** an ordinary board.
- **cyl:** a cylinder. File a and the last file are glued. Ranks are not glued.

## Pieces and moves

- White uses upper case `K Q R B N P`; Black uses lower case `k q r b n p`.
- Queens, rooks, bishops and knights are called *officers*.
- Each side has exactly one king.

Standard chess moves, with these clarifications:

1. **Wraparound (cyl only).**
   - Every horizontal or diagonal step wraps from the last file to file a and back.
   - This applies to kings, knights, sliders and pawn captures. For example, on a cylinder of
     width 5, a white pawn on e2 captures on a3 and on d3.
2. **Sliders.**
   - A slider moves along a ray until it hits the board edge (top or bottom rank), the first
     occupied square, or its own starting square.
   - It may capture an enemy non-king piece on the first occupied square. It never moves through
     a piece.
   - When a ray wraps all the way round and returns to the starting square, the ray stops there.
     There is no null move.
   - On a cylinder the leftward and rightward rays along a rank can reach the same square. That
     is one move, not two.
3. **Pawns.**
   - White pawns move towards rank H; Black pawns move towards rank 1.
   - Single step to an empty square.
   - Double step from the pawn's second rank (rank 2 for White, rank H−1 for Black) if both
     squares are empty. On a 4-rank board the double step lands on the last rank: it then promotes
     (one move per allowed piece) and leaves no e.p. right.
   - A pawn captures diagonally forward. It may capture enemy pawns and enemy officers, never the
     king.
   - A white pawn may never stand on rank 1 or rank H; a black pawn may never stand on rank H or
     rank 1.
4. **Promotion.**
   - A pawn reaching the last rank must promote to one piece of the allowed promotion set.
   - The default set is Q, R, B, N; option `--promote` can restrict it, for example to `Q`.
   - Each allowed piece is a separate move.
5. **En passant** (option `--ep on|off`, default on).
   - Right after a pawn double-steps, the opponent may capture it en passant on their very next
     move only.
   - The capture works exactly as in chess, diagonally across the seam on a cylinder.
   - A position therefore carries an e.p. target square or `-`.
   - With `--ep off` there is no en passant at all.
6. **Kings.**
   - A king moves one step in any direction, with wraparound on a cylinder.
   - It may capture an enemy pawn or officer.
7. **Legality.**
   - A move may not leave the mover's own king attacked.
   - Kings may never be adjacent; this follows from that rule.
   - A position is **illegal** if two pieces share a square, the kings are adjacent, a pawn stands
     on its first or last rank, or the side *not* to move is in check.
   - Illegal input positions are reported as `X`.
8. **Results.**
   - Checkmate (no legal move, and in check) is a loss for the side to move.
   - Stalemate (no legal move, not in check) is a draw.
   - There is no 50-move rule and no castling.
   - A game that can go on forever under best play is a draw. The value is the usual retrograde
     least fixed point:
     - won if some move reaches a lost position;
     - lost if every move reaches a won position;
     - everything else is drawn.

## The officer cap and bounds

(A promotion is capped only if the position *after* it holds more than C officers, so a pawn that
captures an officer while promoting is not capped at count C.)

Exactness can be expensive, so the solvers take `--cap C`, default 1. This is the maximum number of
officers (both colours together) a position may hold.

- A promotion that would create officer number C+1 is a **capped promotion**.
- The solver computes two bounds.
  - **Bound A:** every capped promotion counts as an immediate win for **White**, whoever promoted.
  - **Bound B:** every capped promotion counts as an immediate win for **Black**.
- Results are monotone in terminal values, so A ≥ true value ≥ B from White's point of view.
- When A and B agree, the value is **exact**.
- Input positions may hold at most C officers.

## Position format (one per line)

```
<piece tokens> <side to move> <e.p. target>
```

- Piece tokens are a piece letter and a square, separated by spaces, in any order, for example
  `Ke1 ke3 Pa2 Pb3 pc4 Qd5`.
- The side to move is `w` or `b`.
- The e.p. target is the square that was skipped by the double step (as in FEN), or `-`.
- A line starting with `#` is a comment.

Example (5x5 cylinder): `Kc1 kc5 Pa2 pe4 w -`

## Command line

```
<solver> --board cyl:7x6 --ep on --promote QRBN --cap 1 < positions.txt > results.txt
```

The same interface applies to `kings/kpsolve` and to `python3 -m kings.ref.solve`.

## Output format (one line per input line, same order; comments are copied)

```
<the input position exactly as given> | <A> <B>
```

- `<A>` and `<B>` are each `W` (White wins), `D` (draw), `L` (Black wins) or `X` (illegal).
- They are given from **White's** point of view, whoever is to move.
- Exact when A == B.
/* kpsolve.c -- exact solver for chess positions with kings, pawns and promoted
 * officers (Q, R, B, N) on a small flat or cylindrical board.
 * Rules and file formats: SPEC.md. Written independently of kings/ref/.
 *
 * Method. Positions are grouped into layers by (white pawns, black pawns,
 * officer types). Pawn moves, captures and promotions leave the layer and never
 * come back, so a layer is solved after every layer it can reach (on demand,
 * recursively). Inside a layer only kings and officers move, reversibly, so
 * every placement of them is solved together: counting retrograde analysis on
 * the explicit in-layer move graph, or repeated passes for very large layers.
 * Values are win / loss / draw for the side to move; whatever is never resolved
 * is a draw (the least fixed point, i.e. infinite play is a draw).
 *
 * Extra options beyond SPEC.md, for experiments:
 *   --frozen   kings may not move: a king move loses at once for the mover, and
 *              a promotion wins at once for the promoter (the pawn-only
 *              convention with real races instead of "passed pawn = win")
 *   --moves    after the result, list every legal move with the value it leads to
 *   --threads  worker threads for large layers (default 1)
 */
#include <ctype.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>

#define MAXOFF 3
#define MAXMOVES 320
#define BIT(s) (1ULL << (s))
#define PASS_THRESHOLD 6000000u /* layers with more states use repeated passes */

enum { VUNK = 0, VWIN, VLOSS, VDRAW, VILL };
enum { WHITE = 0, BLACK = 1 };
enum { TQ = 1, TR, TB, TN }; /* officer code = type + 8 * colour */
static const char TYPECH[] = "?QRBN";

static int W, H, N, WRAP, EPON = 1, CAP = 1, FROZEN = 0, BOUND = 0, cappedSeen = 0, WANTMOVES = 0;
static int promoType[4], nPromo;

/* ---------- geometry ---------- */

static int kingN[64], kingL[64][8], knightN[64], knightL[64][8];
static uint64_t kingBB[64], knightBB[64];
static uint64_t pawnAtt[2][64]; /* squares from which a pawn of colour c attacks s */
static int capN[2][64], capL[2][64][2];
static const int DF[8] = {0, 0, 1, -1, 1, 1, -1, -1};
static const int DR[8] = {1, -1, 0, 0, 1, -1, 1, -1};
static int rayN[64][8], rayL[64][8][8];
static int8_t rayIdx[64][8][64];
static uint64_t rayBefore[64][8][8];

static int step(int s, int df, int dr) {
  int f = s % W + df, r = s / W + dr;
  if (r < 0 || r >= H) return -1;
  if (WRAP) f = ((f % W) + W) % W;
  else if (f < 0 || f >= W) return -1;
  return r * W + f;
}

static void add_unique(int *n, int *l, int s) {
  for (int i = 0; i < *n; i++)
    if (l[i] == s) return;
  l[(*n)++] = s;
}

static void init_geometry(void) {
  static const int NF[8] = {1, 2, -1, -2, 1, 2, -1, -2};
  static const int NR[8] = {2, 1, 2, 1, -2, -1, -2, -1};
  memset(rayIdx, -1, sizeof rayIdx);
  memset(pawnAtt, 0, sizeof pawnAtt);
  for (int s = 0; s < N; s++) {
    kingN[s] = knightN[s] = 0;
    kingBB[s] = knightBB[s] = 0;
    for (int d = 0; d < 8; d++) {
      int t = step(s, DF[d], DR[d]);
      if (t >= 0 && t != s) add_unique(&kingN[s], kingL[s], t);
      t = step(s, NF[d], NR[d]);
      if (t >= 0 && t != s) add_unique(&knightN[s], knightL[s], t);
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < kingN[s]; i++) kingBB[s] |= BIT(kingL[s][i]);
    for (int i = 0; i < knightN[s]; i++) knightBB[s] |= BIT(knightL[s][i]);
    for (int d = 0; d < 8; d++) {
      int cur = s;
      uint64_t before = 0;
      rayN[s][d] = 0;
      for (;;) {
        cur = step(cur, DF[d], DR[d]);
        if (cur < 0 || cur == s) break; /* edge, or back home: no null move */
        int k = rayN[s][d]++;
        rayL[s][d][k] = cur;
        rayIdx[s][d][cur] = (int8_t)k;
        rayBefore[s][d][k] = before;
        before |= BIT(cur);
      }
    }
    for (int c = 0; c < 2; c++) {
      capN[c][s] = 0;
      for (int df = -1; df <= 1; df += 2) {
        int t = step(s, df, c == WHITE ? 1 : -1);
        if (t >= 0 && t != s) add_unique(&capN[c][s], capL[c][s], t);
      }
    }
  }
  for (int c = 0; c < 2; c++)
    for (int s = 0; s < N; s++)
      for (int i = 0; i < capN[c][s]; i++) pawnAtt[c][capL[c][s][i]] |= BIT(s);
}

/* ---------- positions ---------- */

typedef struct {
  uint64_t wp, bp;
  int wk, bk, no, stm, ep;
  int osq[MAXOFF], ot[MAXOFF];
} Pos;

typedef struct {
  uint64_t wp, bp;
  int no;
  int ot[MAXOFF];
} Key;

static int colour_of(int code) { return code >> 3; }
static int type_of(int code) { return code & 7; }

static uint64_t occupancy(const Pos *p) {
  uint64_t o = p->wp | p->bp | BIT(p->wk) | BIT(p->bk);
  for (int j = 0; j < p->no; j++) o |= BIT(p->osq[j]);
  return o;
}

static int man_attacks(int type, int from, int target, uint64_t occ) {
  if (type == TN) return (int)((knightBB[from] >> target) & 1);
  int lo = type == TB ? 4 : 0, hi = type == TR ? 4 : 8;
  for (int d = lo; d < hi; d++) {
    int k = rayIdx[from][d][target];
    if (k >= 0 && !(rayBefore[from][d][k] & occ)) return 1;
  }
  return 0;
}

/* is square s attacked by colour `by`? */
static int attacked(const Pos *p, int s, int by) {
  int k = by == BLACK ? p->bk : p->wk;
  if ((kingBB[k] >> s) & 1) return 1;
  if ((by == BLACK ? p->bp : p->wp) & pawnAtt[by][s]) return 1;
  uint64_t occ = occupancy(p);
  for (int j = 0; j < p->no; j++)
    if (colour_of(p->ot[j]) == by && p->osq[j] != s && man_attacks(type_of(p->ot[j]), p->osq[j], s, occ))
      return 1;
  return 0;
}

static int in_check(const Pos *p, int side) {
  return attacked(p, side == BLACK ? p->bk : p->wk, !side);
}

static void canonicalize(Pos *p) {
  for (int i = 1; i < p->no; i++)
    for (int j = i; j > 0; j--) {
      int a = p->ot[j - 1] * 64 + p->osq[j - 1], b = p->ot[j] * 64 + p->osq[j];
      if (a <= b) break;
      int t = p->ot[j]; p->ot[j] = p->ot[j - 1]; p->ot[j - 1] = t;
      t = p->osq[j]; p->osq[j] = p->osq[j - 1]; p->osq[j - 1] = t;
    }
}

static void key_of(const Pos *p, Key *k) {
  memset(k, 0, sizeof *k);
  k->wp = p->wp;
  k->bp = p->bp;
  k->no = p->no;
  for (int j = 0; j < p->no; j++) k->ot[j] = p->ot[j];
}

static int key_eq(const Key *a, const Key *b) {
  if (a->wp != b->wp || a->bp != b->bp || a->no != b->no) return 0;
  for (int j = 0; j < a->no; j++)
    if (a->ot[j] != b->ot[j]) return 0;
  return 1;
}

static int pos_in_key(const Pos *p, const Key *k) {
  if (p->wp != k->wp || p->bp != k->bp || p->no != k->no) return 0;
  for (int j = 0; j < p->no; j++)
    if (p->ot[j] != k->ot[j]) return 0;
  return 1;
}

static size_t layer_size(int no) {
  size_t n = 2;
  for (int i = 0; i < 2 + no; i++) n *= (size_t)N;
  return n;
}

static size_t pos_index(const Pos *p) {
  size_t i = (size_t)p->stm;
  i = i * N + p->wk;
  i = i * N + p->bk;
  for (int j = 0; j < p->no; j++) i = i * N + p->osq[j];
  return i;
}

static void pos_decode(const Key *k, size_t idx, Pos *p) {
  p->wp = k->wp;
  p->bp = k->bp;
  p->no = k->no;
  p->ep = -1;
  for (int j = k->no - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
    p->osq[j] = (int)(idx % N);
    idx /= N;
    p->ot[j] = k->ot[j];
  }
  p->bk = (int)(idx % N);
  idx /= N;
  p->wk = (int)(idx % N);
  idx /= N;
  p->stm = (int)idx;
}

/* legal as a stored state: no overlaps, kings apart, canonical officer order,
 * side not to move not in check (pawn ranks are guaranteed by the key) */
static int legal_state(const Pos *p) {
