# Independent proof attempt: Lemma 2.9

## Claim and convention

Let the side to move be \\(s\\), and let \\(\\bar s\\)'s pawns occupy one file \\(f\\). I use the rank order from \\(\\bar s\\)'s side toward promotion, so “above” means strictly ahead in \\(s\\)'s direction. I treat an attack symmetrically as stipulated: adjacent pawns attack each other, and the side to move may capture. A carried en-passant right was created by a double-step of \\(\\bar s\\).

The blockade strategy is to keep the king on file \\(f\\), in front of the frontmost \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn, and to use a pawn capture whenever an \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn can be removed without breaking that invariant. On any turn when no such capture is available, pass. The permitted pass is important: it lets \\(s\\) leave all timing decisions to \\(\\bar s\\), without advancing an \\(s\\)-pawn into a capture square or moving the king off the barrier.

## Invariant argument

The invariant is: (i) the king of \\(s\\) remains on \\(f\\), ahead of every remaining \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn; (ii) no \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn has crossed the king's rank; and (iii) if an \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn is exposed to an \\(s\\)-pawn capture, \\(s\\) captures it on its next turn. The king's square blocks the forward move of the front pawn. Every other \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn on \\(f\\) is behind that pawn and cannot pass it. The only way a pawn can leave the file is a diagonal capture. By (B1), at most one \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn is attacked by an \\(s\\)-pawn at a time; hence \\(\\bar s\\) cannot use one capture to open a second, simultaneous pawn escape while the first is being taken. After the capture, the number of \\(\\bar s\\)-pawns on \\(f\\) decreases and the same barrier applies to the remaining stack. If no capture is currently available, a pass preserves the position and clears any en-passant right.

An \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn that captures an \\(s\\)-pawn diagonally leaves \\(f\\), but the move is still exposed to the stipulated mutual pawn attack: it is the unique attacked pawn allowed by (B1), and the next move by \\(s\\) takes it. Thus it cannot become a lasting passer. The capture may be en passant only when \\(s\\) has just double-stepped; in that case the resulting pawn is likewise on a square attacked by the capturing pawn, and the same immediate recapture rule applies. Conversely, if the carried right belongs to \\(\\bar s\\), the first pass clears it. A king capture of an \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn is also safe: it removes a blocker from the stack while the king remains ahead of the next pawn. No capture of a king is legal.

A legal move by \\(\\bar s\\)'s king cannot invalidate the barrier. If it approaches the king of \\(s\\), the kings' non-adjacency rule keeps it from occupying or crossing the barrier square; if it moves away, it only weakens its own pawn support. The king of \\(s\\) need not chase it: the strategy uses a stationary barrier and passes. If \\(s\\)'s king is in check on its turn, it cannot pass. A checking \\(\\bar s\\)-king cannot be adjacent to the barrier king, and a pawn check from the blocked file can only be produced by the unique attacked pawn in (B1); capturing that pawn (or, if required by legality, moving the king one square along the file while staying ahead of the front pawn) answers the check. The remaining cases are checks by an \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn which has left \\(f\\); that pawn is the exposed pawn described above and can be captured. If none of these replies is legal, this invariant argument has a gap; the small-board test below is intended to check this point.

The argument is independent of the distance from promotion. At \\(H=4\\), the rank convention places \\(\\bar s\\)'s pawns on ranks 2 or higher counting from its side. Two attacked pawns would require one \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn on rank 3 or higher attacked by an \\(s\\)-pawn on rank 4, the last rank for \\(s\\); such an \\(s\\)-pawn cannot exist. Thus (B1) is automatic on four-rank boards. For \\(H\\ge5\\), two attacked pawns are geometrically possible: an \\(s\\)-pawn may stand on an interior rank and attack two successive pawns of the file stack. (B1) excludes exactly this fork, in which the opponent can take one pawn and leave the other unanswerable. With (B1), no \\(\\bar s\\)-pawn ever reaches its last rank. The same invariant prevents a mating net: \\(s\\) can always pass when not in check and can answer a check as above, so \\(s\\) is never mated.

## Status

This is an attempted proof, not yet independently certified. In particular, the response to check must be verified against all legal pawn captures and king placements; I do not regard the informal “one exposed pawn” paragraph as a substitute for that case analysis. The exhaustive safety-game check requested in the brief is recorded separately in "'`FINDINGS.md` after Phase 2.
