143-## 0. Summary
144-
145-**The question.** The convention scores a passed pawn as ±BIG (BIG = 256), sums pawn components, and treats
146-the king group Z (the trébuchet) as the game 0. When does this give the chess result?
147-
148-**The answer, in one paragraph.** The convention gives the chess result when four conditions hold, together
149-with the per-position hypothesis (A_Q)(X) that a won promotion race is a won game (Theorem 1.14; (A_Q) is
150-false as a general statement, so it is checked position by position by kpsolve; revised after review: R1 item
151-6, R2 item 8):
152-- (V1) the king group never interacts with the components, nobody is ever stalemated, and en passant obeys
153-  condition (E) of L1.6 in `notes/lemmas.md`;
154-- (V2) every "decisive event" really decides the game;
155-- (V3) every move inside Z is decisively punished;
156-- (V4) no two components carry threats of opposite sign, and BIG exceeds every tempo count. The check is
157-  hereditary, on the components of every reachable position, and a threat is a move into a terminal by either
158-  player (revised after review: R1 item 1, R2 items 1 and 2).
159-
160-Only (V4) concerns BIG. Without it, +BIG and −BIG cancel and no value of BIG helps (Proposition 1.12).
161-The other three are chess facts.
162-- For a king move, (V2)+(V3) reduce to a local certificate: the punisher promotes within T moves against a
163-  defender who may pass, and the defender cannot promote within T moves elsewhere (Theorem 2.7: PROVED after
164-  repair, in P; it gives a P-win, not a C-win; revised after K7, 2026-10-22). The counter-promotion half
165-  counts the defender's king and context pawns only: its Z-pawns are covered by the local game. That half holds
166-  in every chess-legal non-terminal context when T = 1, on boards with at least 5 ranks (Proposition 2.10(a)).
167-  For T ≥ 2 it fails in some context on boards at least |F_Z| + 4 files wide (Proposition 2.10(b)),
168-  so a slower punishment's zero is not certified in every context. That it is context-dependent is CHECKED-2 for
169-  Z_T in P (Z_T beside {Pe3, pe4} and {Pe2, pe3}, three implementations agree, no hand proof, P13); for every
170-  frozen group with T ≥ 2 it is a CONJECTURE (revised after K23, 2026-10-27).
171-- When a king has a safe square to shuttle to, Z is not 0 but the loopy game `on`/`off` (or `dud`). On that
172-  side BIG is meaningless, because `on` exceeds every number. The correct rule replaces ±BIG by
173-  game-ending outcomes (Theorem 3.4):
174-  - **if only White can pass,** Black wins iff Black can force a decisive event against a White who may
175-    pass at any time; otherwise White wins;
176-  - **if both sides can pass,** a side wins iff it can force a decisive event against a passing opponent;
177-    otherwise the game is drawn. For a king group with two frozen shuttles this is Proposition 3.2′ together with
178-    Theorem 3.4(a), in the D-model; in chess it also needs (Pun) and (A_Q), Theorem 3.2‴ (revised after K14,
179-    2026-10-27).
