218-## 1. Chess semantics versus CGT semantics
219-
220-### 1.1 Notation
221-
222-- **Board.** W files × H ranks, flat or cylinder, with the rules of `kings/SPEC.md`. d(x, y) is the king
223-  distance: max(file distance, rank distance), with cyclic file distance on a cylinder.
224-- **Players.** White = Left, Black = Right.
225-- **Result pairs.** (r_w, r_b) lists the result with White to move, then with Black to move. Each entry is
226-  W, D or L **from White's point of view**, as kpsolve prints them.
--
310-### 1.3 Two lemmas about options
311-
312-**Lemma 1.6 (option monotonicity). PROVED.** Let A′ be obtained from a D-game A by adding Left options or
313-deleting Right options at some positions. Then r_{A′}(x, s) ≥ r_A(x, s) for all x and s, in the order
314-L < D < W.
315-
316-*Proof.*
317-- Every White strategy in A is a White strategy in A′, because White has at least the same options there.
318-- Every Black reply in A′ is also available in A, because Black's options there are a subset.
319-- So a strategy that forces a win in A forces it in A′, and a strategy that avoids losing in A avoids losing
320-  in A′. ∎
321-
322-**Lemma 1.7 (suicide deletion, normal play only). PROVED.** Deleting a Left option equal to ⊖ (a "suicide")
323-does not change any result. The same holds for Right options equal to ⊕.
324-
325-*Proof.* r(x, Left) is the best result over Left's options, and a move to ⊖ yields L, the worst.
326-- If Left has another option, the maximum is unchanged.
327-- If ⊖ was Left's only option, deleting it leaves Left with no option. Under normal play Left then loses:
328-  the result is L again. ∎
329-
330:**Remark 1.8 (what the trébuchet is for).** In chess the second case of Lemma 1.7 fails: a side with no
331-legal move is **stalemated**, which is a draw.
332-- The convention's normal-play rule "a side with no pawn move loses" is therefore correct in chess only
333-  because the king group always offers that side a legal move, and that move is a suicide.
334-- The trébuchet's real job is to turn stalemate into a loss.
335-- §5.4 gives a position where a context pawn takes away the only suicide square. The convention says
336-  "White wins" and the truth is a draw.
337-
338-### 1.4 What BIG can and cannot do
339-
340-**Theorem 1.9 (one component). PROVED.** For a short D-game A and every B ≥ 1, the outcome class of A^B at
341-x equals the D-result pair of A at x.
342-
343-*Proof.* Induction on the height of x.
344-- The integer B ≥ 1 is in 𝓛. Moving first, Left moves to B − 1 ≥ 0, where Right has no move. Moving second,
345-  Right has no move at all.
346-- Likewise −B is in 𝓡.
347-- So an option to ⊕ (resp. ⊖) in A corresponds to an option in 𝓛 (resp. 𝓡) in A^B. That is exactly
348-  "White wins (resp. loses) from it whoever moves next".
349-- An option to a non-terminal y has matching classes by induction.
350-- "Left wins moving first" holds iff some Left option is won by Left with Right to move, in both games.
351-  The same holds for Right.
352-- A player with no option loses in both games. ∎
353-
354-In one component of the D-model the convention cannot go wrong: after the first ±B nothing else happens.
355-Trouble needs at least two components (compare L2.3 of `notes/lemmas.md`). That is *not* true of the
356-re-splitting model S of §1.5 (revised after review: R2 item 2): there a single component can still fail, because
357-a passer in one sub-part does not end its sibling, so trouble needs a later split, not two components. Example:
358-flat 7×6, e.p. on, count rule, `W: b2 d3 f2 / B: c4 c5 e5 g5` is one component at the root, yet o(S) = 𝓝 while
359-the decisive-event result is 𝓡 (COMPUTED-1, `lemmas/common.py`).
360-
361-**Definition 1.10 (tempo counts, threats, dominance).** Let Y = (y₁, …, y_k) be a position of a sum of short
362-D-games.
363-- **Tempo counts.** M_L(y) is the largest number of Left moves in any sequence of moves from y (moves by
364-  both sides, in any order, not necessarily alternating), counting a final move into a terminal. M_R(y) is
365-  defined the same way for Right. Both are monotone: M(y′) ≤ M(y) whenever y′ is reachable from y.
