1067-**Lemma 2.7a (transfer from a promotion model to chess). PROVED (new, revised after K24, 2026-10-27).** For a
1068-king-and-pawn position X let EPW(X) be chess in which the first promotion by either side ends the game as a White win,
1069-and EPB(X) the same with a Black win. Everything else is as in chess: a mate wins, stalemate and infinite play are
1070-draws. A promotion move is legal in EPW and EPB exactly when it is legal in chess.
1071-- (a) If White has no winning strategy in EPW(X), White has none in C(X).
1072-- (b) If Black has no winning strategy in EPB(X), Black has none in C(X).
1073-- (c) If both hold, C(X) is a draw.
1074-- *Proof.* (a) By contraposition. Let σ be a winning strategy of White in C(X). Every play consistent with σ is
1075-  finite and ends in a mate of Black. Play σ in EPW(X), against any behaviour of Black. Until the first promotion the
1076-  legal moves, the mate test and the stalemate test are the same in the two games, so the play so far is a prefix of
1077-  a chess play consistent with σ. If a promotion (by either side) occurs before σ's mate, the play ends in EPW as a
1078-  White win. If none occurs, the whole play is a chess play consistent with σ and ends in the mate of Black, which
1079-  is a White win in EPW as well. No play is infinite. So σ wins EPW(X). (b) is the same with the colours exchanged.
1080-  (c) EPW, EPB and C are finite-state reachability games, so "neither side can force a win" is exactly the value D.
1081-  ∎
1082-- The lemma is used only in the direction "cannot win". The converse is not claimed (White may win EPW by a
1083-  promotion that chess does not reward). EPW and EPB do not distinguish the piece promoted to, and the proof needs
1084-  no cap on officers.
