2730-## 7. Trying to break the proofs
2731-
2732-Each item is a counterexample type the task named, or one found while writing.
2733-
2734-1. **A zugzwang where the mover can make a decisive threat elsewhere first.**
2735-   - In the D-model a mover with a decisive option is not in zugzwang, so nothing breaks there.
2736-   - In P the threat may be too slow. In 7.3, a Black passer elsewhere loses to f8=Q.
2737-   - That is a (V2) failure for the passer, not a gap in Theorems 1.14 or 2.4, which assume (V2). With
2738-     frozen kings, the certificate of Proposition 2.11 fails there at (H5), the timing bound over all of the opponent's pawns (a failed
2739-     certificate does not by itself prove a (V2) failure: 2.11 is sufficient only);
2740:     with real kings the square-rule conjecture of Remark 2.11 is FALSE as stated (ω can be stalemated; CHECKED-2), and Theorem 2.11K (PROVED in P: a corrected rule with a no-stalemate condition) applies where its hypotheses hold (revised after K16, 2026-10-27).
2741-2. **Double threats.**
2742-   - They are just Λ-positions in Theorem 3.4: every reply leaves a threat. No proof uses "one threat at a
2743-     time".
2744-   - Theorem 1.11 needs pairwise safety only *between* components. Several threats inside one component are
2745-     allowed.
2746-3. **Stalemate.**
2747-   - It broke the naive argument: Lemma 1.7 is false under chess rules.
2748-   - Hence (V1b) in Theorem 1.14, (L3) in Definition 2.6, the unlimited-pass clause in (L1) (the earlier "every budget" phrasing is withdrawn; revised after K7, 2026-10-22), and the traps
2749-     of §5.4.
2750-   - Remaining gap: (L3) is not checked for P4.
2751-4. **En passant.**
2752-   - Lemma 2.2: an en passant capture lands on the captured pawn's file, so it never crosses the buffer.
2753-   - Inside components, the sum needs condition (E) (L1.6–1.7 of `notes/lemmas.md`). Theorem 1.14(V1a)
2754-     assumes it.
2755-   - The scratch solver ignores en passant. In every local game it was used on, the defender had no pawns,
2756-     so no en passant capture exists (PROVED: it needs a pawn of the capturing side).
2757-   - In the full positions of §6 no double step lands beside an enemy pawn on the same rank, so no en passant
2758-     right ever arises.
2759-5. **A king that leaves Z and catches a passer.**
2760-   - That is a Z-move made while a context passer exists, so the D-model has already ended. The failure is
2761-     (V2) for the passer.
2762-   - Proposition 2.11 is proved only with frozen kings. With real kings the defender's king may catch the
2763-     passer (`Ka1 ka6 Pc2 w -` is (W, W) frozen and (D, D) with real kings); the real-king form is Theorem 2.11K of
2764:     Remark 2.11 (the square rule with a no-stalemate clause, PROVED in P; without the clause the rule is FALSE, CHECKED-2, K16) (revised after K16, 2026-10-27).
2765-   - The engine-suite escapes (§5.6) are this type. Theorem 2.7 explains them only as certificate failures;
2766-     it does not claim to predict which ones draw.
2767-6. **Checks.**
2768-   - Context pawns never attack F_Z (Lemma 2.2).
2769-   - Inside Z, checks are part of Z's own move lists. The scratch geometry includes legality under check.
2770-7. **Extra moves of the winner.**
2771-   - Z_onT1 gives White king moves to g4–g6 that are not shuttle moves. The sharp form of Theorem 1.14 covers
2772-     the winner: those moves are simply not played, and Lemma 1.6 is not needed (revised after K9, 2026-10-22).
2773-   - When Black is the winner (P9, Black to move), Black's forced line was checked against *all* White moves.
2774-     Black promotes on e1 at its second move, and no White king square is within reach of e1.
2775-8. **Is the 2-cycle really equivalent to a self-loop?**
2776-   - The bisimulation in Proposition 3.2 needs *both* shuttle states to have identical properties. For two shuttles
2777-     (Proposition 3.2′) it needs all four combined states, with both shuttles legal in each; deleting any one of the
2778-     eight arrows changes the result (Proposition 3.2′(d)), and the kings' opposition, a missing diagonal state, gives
2779-     (W, L) instead of (D, D).
